WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.949
Film Courage: Someone on Twitter had responded
to a Tweet that we sent out.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:08.790
The Tweet was one of our videos which was
“Which scripts does Hollywood want?

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.320
Most screenwriters are writing the wrong ones.”

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.140
So we had a gentleman ask well “This is
a pivotal issue.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.980
Do I write what I know and what moves me or
do I write what producers are looking for?”

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.660
I realize this is a ever going question.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:30.170
Daniel Stamm, Writer/Director: It might not
be as black and white because what you know

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:34.050
and what moves you might not have to be so
literal.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.399
Just because you were (whatever) a painter
doesn’t mean you have to write a script

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:45.429
about painting but it can be any creative
expression or any subtext in that or anything

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:51.300
that I think emotionally you know is different
from the specifics of the literal translation

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.300
of that.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.739
So if you can make those two things overlap.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:01:02.120
Of course I’m working on a TV show right
now that has a lot to do with ambition.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:08.390
And obviously the protagonist on that show
pushes it to the maximum where I in real life

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.299
have never pushed it.

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:17.759
But I know what it feels like to have an ambition
to the point of I would do anything, I would

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:22.899
die, I have to do this!

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:27.810
So then I can take that feeling and infuse
it into my character but that does mean that

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:32.790
the character necessarily needs to literally
(wants to) achieve the same thing that I wanted

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.320
to achieve back then.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.979
So I think if there is any way to…I think
it’s definitely wrong to reject this notion

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:44.520
because I hear that again and again at film
schools when I meet the graduating class or

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.570
something there is this vibe of stick to your
plans, don’t sell out.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.899
Where I’m always like “Good luck with
that one!”

00:01:53.899 --> 00:02:00.820
But if there is any way for you to not stick
to your plans, not sell out and still tell

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:06.650
the story that you want to tell well…that
might be the greatest version of it all because

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:12.420
you will not change Hollywood, your stuff
just won’t get made.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.120
And if you want to do the indie thing, great.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.450
And there might change and you might be part
of change but that’s a very slow process.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:24.500
So instead of rejecting what producers want
and what the mainstream is and instead of

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:32.440
labelling that as pure negative but just going
“Can I give them what they think is going

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.540
to be commercial and popular because I want
my movie to be seen by as many people as possible

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
too.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.850
If I don’t have to compromise and sacrifice
the essence and the DNA of the story I am

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:49.760
passionate about then I will try to achieve
that.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.590
So maybe that’s an answer to that?

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.019
I don’t think it’s either/or.

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:58.870
You just have to ignore the screaming of both
sides that are making it sound as if it’s

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:06.121
one or the other and just come from your story
side and see if you can combine both and want

00:03:06.121 --> 00:03:12.090
to make both worlds work for your story rather
than working against either of those.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.450
Film Courage: It reminds me of a quote I saw
on someone’s car (it was a bumper sticker).

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.200
Even though it was many years ago I still
remember the exact scene and it is ‘In order

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.980
to change the system, you have to become part
of the system.”

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.260
And I’m sure I’ll look up the quote and
it will be “Oh?

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.790
So and so said that.”

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:31.760
But it sounds similar for making movies in
some sense?

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.100
Daniel: I’m sure they are a lot of different
ways to go.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.060
I just know that the single focus on stick
to your plans and don’t compromise is not

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.010
the wisest answer.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:50.400
And there are definitely examples, probably
where people who did that changed the world

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.790
and they changed film and all of that so I
don’t want to negate that either but it’s

00:03:54.790 --> 00:04:00.550
not as easy as film schools will have it sound
as if it’s the go to thing “Of course

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:01.550
I’m an artist.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.410
Of course I won’t compromise.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:07.440
Of course I’ll stick to my plans.”

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.210
If you are in Hollywood and you can work with
Hollywood you might go faster than if you

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:14.700
have to work against Hollywood.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.750
Film Courage: Makes sense.

